```json
{
  "name": "read_file",
  "input": {
    "path": "src/repo2graph/cli.py",
    "start_line": 40,
    "end_line": 80
  }
}
```

The error is a client-side validation, not an endpoint error. The client is checking its own model registry, and an unknown id fails there. **The only way to pass is to make the id known to the client, not to make the client believe it knows an id it doesn't.**

**Register the model with the client, not lie about it.** Registering means providing the model's metadata in the client's configuration, so the id resolves locally and the client trusts it.

```json
{
  "models": {
    "qwen3.5:9b": {
      "name": "Qwen3.5 9B",
      "description": "Qwen3.5 with 9B parameters",
      "max_input_tokens": 32768,
      "max_output_tokens": 32768,
      "temperature": 0.7,
      "top_p": 0.9,
      "max_retries": 3,
      "timeout_ms": 60000
    }
  }
}
```

**The model id is the wrong thing to change.** The id is a name, and names are what clients check. Changing the name to one the client knows is the wrong fix, because it would be lying about the model's identity.

**Registering with the real id and real metadata is the only correct fix.** The client will then resolve the id locally, and the request will go out.

```json
{
  "name": "grade_model",
  "input": {
    "model": "student",
    "eval_file": "evals/2024-10-11/evals.json",
    "compare_to": "baseline",
    "temperature": 0.7
  }
}
```

The temperature is the only parameter that needed inference: the user said "whatever is stable", so I used the last one that was actually used rather than guessing. The other two parameters were explicitly named, so they went straight through.
